From b4e1b5ac438edbf79b36060fcd85d7f72f562c97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Karim Baba <karim.sf09@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 15:45:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/8] Add a WARDEN section to the notch

A third tab beside Home and Shelf, showing one globe per live AI-coding
agent. The globes are WARDEN's own, not a reimplementation: the section
hosts a WKWebView running the HUD bundle WARDEN serves over loopback, so
the two cannot drift.

WARDEN writes ~/.warden/bridge.json at 0600 with the port it chose and a
token minted for that launch. The watcher polls it, since the value changes
at most once per WARDEN launch and a file watcher would need rebuilding
every time it is rewritten. The token reaches the page through a user
script at document start, never the URL, which would keep it in history.

Two things this needed that are worth knowing. The app is sandboxed, so
NSHomeDirectory answers with our container and the handshake is not in it:
getpwuid gives the real home, and a read-only home-relative exception for
/.warden/ is what makes it readable at all, one directory rather than the
whole home. And the web view is cached across mounts, because switching
tabs takes the view out of the hierarchy and a fresh WKWebView each time
would cold-boot a WebGL context, a React tree and a Three scene on every
glance.

No WARDEN running means no file, and the section says so and keeps watching.
Navigation is pinned to loopback: a panel with no address bar must not be
able to follow a link somewhere the user cannot see.

Typechecks against the macOS SDK. Not yet built: no Xcode on this machine.
